    "result_text": "The full test suite passes \u2014 **46 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**, including the new `Cp33Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented rejection of a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date, returning HTTP 400, following the existing Spring conventions in the codebase.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New exception** \u2014 `rest/controller/FutureRegistrationDateException.java`: a `RuntimeException` carrying the supplied date and the server date, mirroring the existing domain exceptions (e.g. `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException`).\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a `rejectFutureRegistrationDate(...)` guard called right after postcode validation, **before** the null-default and business-day roll. This ensures:\n   - Only a *supplied* date is checked (a `null` date defaults to the server date and is accepted).\n   - The comparison is against the raw supplied value (`isAfter(LocalDate.now())`), not the weekend-rolled value, so it faithfully rejects future-dated registrations.\n\n3. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added a `@ExceptionHandler` for `FutureRegistrationDateException` returning `400 Bad Request` with a `ProblemDetail` and `errors: [\"registrationDate\"]`, consistent with the other 400 handlers (invalid email/postcode/telephone).\n\nThe new `Cp33Tests.errorRejectsFutureDate` (posts `registrationDate: \"2999-01-01\"`, expects 400) passes, and all 45 pre-existing tests remain green.",
